The first version of the progress service (NestJS), before the outbox:

async completeLesson(userId: string, lessonId: string) {
  const progress = await this.db.transaction(async (tx) => {
    return tx.progress.addLesson(userId, lessonId);   // INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, add points
  });                                                  // COMMIT
  await this.bus.publish({ type: "LessonCompleted", userId, lessonId, points: progress.points });
  return progress;
}

Production log, pod api-7c9f (stopped by a deploy):
09:14:03.118 tx COMMIT user=u_4410 lesson=l_212
09:14:03.121 SIGTERM received, shutting down
(no "published LessonCompleted" line for u_4410 / l_212, ever)

Statement: "The progress of u_4410 for l_212 is saved, but the leaderboard will never receive this completion unless something re-sends the event: the commit and the publish are two separate writes."

The leaderboard consumer (Redis), deduplicating by event id:

async onLessonCompleted(e: LessonCompleted) {
  const isNew = await redis.sadd("lb:processed", e.eventId);   // 1 if added, 0 if already there
  if (isNew === 1) {
    await redis.zincrby("lb:weekly:2026-W44", e.points, e.userId);
  }
  // the bus acks the message when this function returns
}

Statement: "Because SADD runs before ZINCRBY, a crash between the two commands can at worst make an event count twice; it can never lose one."
